ard deviations that the p/e of the  given stock is below the market average  and that's what we call the z-score so I  grab the mean and the standard deviation  I put them into a data frame of  themselves because I'm going to need  those to calculate the z-score and I  plotted it again I'm going to explore my  data see what's going on it looks kind  of normal to me it's moving around the  standard deviation is less than the mean  that's good and now I can calculate the  z-score the data frame has methods  subtract divide add that sort of thing  so I can take this way I can do in a  single line of code I can do math on  every element in the entire data frame  so I'm subtracting the mean dividing by  the standard deviation that gives me the  z-score I see some of them are very  positive these are stocks whose PE is  very much above the average some are  negative they're below the average I  decided just to look at the max and min  of each stock just to take a look at  what it what it was if it seemed normal  or anything was standing out and I  plotted that so I have the max and min  p/e ratio for every stock everything  looked kind of okay to me here there was  nothing standing out that made me  worried so now we have a way to measure  whether the p/e is high or low  and we need to calculate returns so that  we can compare them formula for return  finding that final value divided by  starting value but if I want to compare  returns for different periods but as I  want three month returns six month  returns it's good to analyze the return  the other thing to know here is that the  final value requires but the final price  and the dividends that were earned  during the period of return that we're  calculating so I have a CSV file with  prices I read that in same issues as  before I cleaned it up a little bit this  also goes back to 1989 there's a couple  of stocks missing prices they weren't  trading yet so I want to get this this  data frame into the same shape as the 